<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.7.1: Factoring Strategies for Polynomials</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01697</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.7.1: Factoring Strategies for Polynomials</md:title>
    <md:uuid>fcc74301-4d19-427f-87e2-4f6cb0e38812</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>

<para>In this unit, remember to double click on mathematical expressions/equations to enlarge, if needed.</para>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Working with a partner, list as many strategies as you can for factoring polynomials. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answers here:</para>
  <para>Your answers may vary, but here are some examples:</para>
    <list>
      <item>Factoring by finding the GCF of an expression and using reverse distribution</item>
      <item>Factoring using grouping</item>
      <item>Factoring trinomials of the form \(x^2 + bx + c\) (undo FOIL)</item>
      <item>Factoring trinomials of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\) using trial and error</item>
      <item>Factoring trinomials using the “\(ac\)” method</item>
      <item>Factoring using substitution</item>
      <item>Factoring the difference of squares</item>
      <item>Factoring perfect square trinomials</item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Work with your partner to write a sample polynomial that would be fitting for each strategy listed. </para>
    <para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
  <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
    <thead>
      <row>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
          Factoring Strategy
        </entry>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
          Sample Polynomial
        </entry>
      </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <row>
        <entry>
          Factoring by finding the GCF of an expression and using reverse distribution
        </entry>
        <entry>
          \(14a + 8ab + 12\)
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>
          Factoring using grouping
        </entry>
        <entry>
          \(x^2 + 2x − 5x − 10\)
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>
          Factoring trinomials of the form \(x^2 + bx + c\) (undo FOIL)
        </entry>
        <entry>
          \(t^2 + 14t + 24\)
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>
          Factoring trinomials of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\) using trial and error
        </entry>
        <entry>
          \(18x^2 − 3xy − 10y^2\)
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>
          Factoring trinomials using the “\(ac\)” method
        </entry>
        <entry>
          \(16x^2 − 32x + 12\)
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>
          Factoring using substitution
        </entry>
        <entry>
          \((x−2)^2 + 7(x−2) + 12\)
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>
          Factoring the difference of squares
        </entry>
        <entry>
          \(81y^2 − 1\)
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>
          Factoring perfect square trinomials
        </entry>
        <entry>
          \(25v^2 + 20v + 4\)
        </entry>
      </row>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
